WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.790
Hey, David, do you consider yourself Hispanic?
What about Latino? What about an immigrant?

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:10.680
Uh, speaking of identity politics, uh, a question
about what is my identity?

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.070
This is like a really, really long thing.
Um, obviously I'm an immigrant in the sense

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:20.150
that I was born in Argentina and I immigrated
to the United States. There's this really

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:25.420
funny story that I'm reminded of. When I was
in middle school, um, there was an assembly

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.931
where they announced we are going to be giving
awards. My earpiece fell out here. Uh, we

00:00:29.931 --> 00:00:34.932
are going to be giving awards for achievement
by Latino students. And all of a sudden they

00:00:34.932 --> 00:00:39.690
say, David Pakman, please stand up. And I
was genuinely confused and I went home and

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:44.110
said to my parents, are we Latino in Spanish?
Of course the language I grew up in still

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:48.930
speak at home. Are We Latino? Are We Hispanic?
What's the difference? So here's my take on,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.040
on this question, not that my identity matters,
but because it is the question that was asked.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.370
I was born in Argentina and I grew up speaking
Spanish.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:02.649
So I am clearly of what I call Hispanic culture
or Argentinian culture. That's the culture

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:08.710
I was raised in. Now, oftentimes there are
racial or ethnic questions tied up in these

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:13.539
questions. I was born to a Jewish family who
moved to Argentina from eastern Europe. Much

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:18.979
like many Jews moved to the United States
before World War II. So my ethnicity is Ashkenazi

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:25.549
Jewish in the United States. Aside from some
white nationalists, I guess Typically Ashkenazi

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:30.759
Jews are considered considered white when
it comes to their race or ethnicity. Had I

00:01:30.759 --> 00:01:35.590
been born in the United States, I would clearly
be considered white by the definitions of

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:41.200
the census. To the extent that Jews are considered
white, so let's maybe dig in a little more

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:48.249
to the like Hispanic Latino thing. One version
of Hispanic versus Latino is that Hispanic

00:01:48.249 --> 00:01:53.049
is everyone from Spanish speaking countries
in south and Latin America and that includes

00:01:53.049 --> 00:01:54.969
Spain in Europe as well.

00:01:54.969 --> 00:02:01.610
Uh, while Latino is all countries that are
physically in south and Central America, but

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:06.499
not Spain, which is in Europe and it's more
complex because you have places like Haiti

00:02:06.499 --> 00:02:11.390
and Martinique and St Martin there, there's
sort of not Latino there. They're not really

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.500
Hispanic by any of these definitions and so
it starts to get a little bit complicated.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:20.110
And then Brazil as a Portuguese speaking a
South American country also, sometimes it's

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:25.340
complex. I actually think that that's basically
a distraction for us. I'm definitely a Hispanic

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.420
immigrant to the u s from Latin America. I
am definitely ethnically Jewish and I pass

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.950
as white in the United States because I'm
ethnically Jewish and I learned English at

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.440
a young enough age so that I don't have an
accent other than some specific words that

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:46.250
slip out every once in awhile. I tend not
to use the term Latino because it does feel

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.820
a little bit tainted in the context of the
immigration debate.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:54.510
But at the same time I feel like Hispanic
is very imprecise because you might be talking

00:02:54.510 --> 00:03:00.740
about a Guatemalan immigrant or a family of
multigenerational wealth in Spain and politically

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:05.380
it's not just class, uh, in terms of money
politically, those are very, very different

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.840
things. And the idea that Hispanic encompass
encompasses all of it. Okay. It just seems

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.710
extraordinarily broad and imprecise anyway.
People have often seen me sort of mess with

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:21.540
my own identity when guests try to use my
perceived identity against me in some way.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:26.180
And I sometimes would have joked with my girlfriend,
I'm not white, but the truth is I'm Hispanic

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.550
by culture. I'm ethnically Jewish, which is
white in the United States for all intents

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:37.730
and purposes. Uh, so I would say yes, I am
Hispanic. Uh, I think, I think I, I guess

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.210
I am Latino if I have to use that phrase,
that term.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:45.430
Although I try not to use it because I feel
like it is stigmatized in the immigration

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:50.569
debate. And I am definitely an immigrant.
My passport will confirm that. Uh, we've got

